## 3. FREE ACCESS TO THE ORIGINAL BASE

### 3.1

The Original Base may be downloaded and used free of charge.

### 3.2

No person may require payment as a condition of obtaining the Original Base.

### 3.3

The Original Base may be redistributed free of charge, provided that this License and all required copyright and attribution notices are retained.

### 3.4

The Original Base must not be presented as a paid product, premium download, exclusive product, or otherwise monetized version of the Original Base.

### 3.5

Nothing in this section prevents You from charging for a genuine Derivative Project that satisfies the requirements of this License.

## 4. COMMERCIALIZATION OF DERIVATIVE PROJECTS

### 4.1

You MAY sell, license, monetize, distribute, or otherwise commercialize a genuine Derivative Project.

### 4.2

Commercialization is permitted only where the project contains meaningful original development beyond the Original Base.

### 4.3

You may charge for your own original additions, functionality, systems, features, design, development, integrations, or other original work incorporated into a Derivative Project.

### 4.4

Permission to commercially distribute a Derivative Project does not grant permission to commercially distribute the Original Base by itself.

### 4.5

The mere inclusion of the Base in a project does not automatically prohibit commercial use. The determining factor is whether the resulting product qualifies as a genuine Derivative Project under this License.

### 4.6

The commercial value of a Derivative Project should primarily come from the original work added by You or Your contributors rather than from the Original Base itself.

## 5. PROHIBITED COMMERCIALIZATION

You MAY NOT:

(a) sell the Original Base by itself;

(b) sell an unchanged copy of the Original Base;

(c) sell a renamed copy of the Original Base;

(d) sell a rebranded copy of the Original Base;

(e) sell the Original Base with only trivial or cosmetic modifications;

(f) place the Original Base behind a paywall;

(g) charge money solely for access to the Original Base;

(h) claim that the Original Base is your own original creation;

(i) remove the original attribution for the purpose of making the Base appear to be your own work;

(j) package the Original Base with insignificant additions primarily to circumvent this License;

(k) describe the Original Base itself as proprietary software owned or created by You;

(l) distribute the Original Base under a license that removes or contradicts the restrictions contained in this License; or

(m) intentionally structure a transaction, distribution method, or product primarily for the purpose of circumventing the restrictions on selling the Original Base.

## 6. WHAT COUNTS AS A DERIVATIVE PROJECT

### 6.1

A Derivative Project must contain meaningful original development created by You or Your contributors.

### 6.2

Examples of meaningful development may include:

(a) substantial new functionality;

(b) new systems or features;

(c) significant architectural changes;

(d) substantial new source code;

(e) substantial user-interface development;

(f) new configuration or management systems;

(g) new integrations;

(h) new tools or systems;

(i) substantial performance or functionality improvements; or

(j) other original development that materially changes or expands the purpose or functionality of the Base.

### 6.3

The following changes, by themselves, will generally NOT constitute sufficient development to create a Derivative Project:

(a) changing the name;

(b) changing the logo;

(c) changing colors;

(d) changing a few strings;

(e) changing advertisements or branding;

(f) changing configuration values;

(g) removing the original attribution;

(h) making minor cosmetic changes;

(i) making insignificant source-code changes; or

(j) performing other changes whose primary purpose is to make the Original Base appear different while retaining substantially the same functionality.

### 6.4

No specific percentage of changed code determines whether a project qualifies as a Derivative Project.

The determination shall instead consider the project as a whole, including:

* the amount and significance of original development;
* the functionality added;
* the extent of architectural or technical changes;
* the originality of the new code and systems; and
* whether the commercial value primarily comes from the Original Base or from the new work created by You.

